      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Toss potato chips in 2 tbsp oil and roast at 220C for 25 minutes, turning once, until golden. Remove and keep warm."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Marinate the beef strips in soy sauce, vinegar, garlic, cumin, and a pinch of salt for 10 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the wok over the highest heat you have. Add remaining oil. When smoking hot, add beef in a single layer. Do not stir for 90 seconds. Then toss for 1 more minute. Remove."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In the same wok, fry the onion wedges for 2 minutes over high heat until they soften at the edges but keep some crunch."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add tomato wedges and chilli. Toss for 1 minute. Return the beef to the wok."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the chips and toss everything together. Scatter with fresh coriander and serve immediately with rice."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Beef is grey and steamed rather than seared",
              "cause": "The wok was not hot enough or the pan was overcrowded.",
              "fix": "Cook the beef in two batches in a very hot wok. Crowding drops the temperature and the meat steams instead of frying."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Chips go soft in the dish",
              "cause": "Chips added too early and absorbed steam from the sauce.",
              "fix": "Add the chips at the very last moment, just before serving. Toss quickly and serve immediately."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Sauce is too salty",
              "cause": "Too much soy sauce or the beef marinated too long.",
              "fix": "Add a squeeze of lime juice to cut the salt. A few tablespoons of water will also dilute it."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Lomo saltado is one of the clearest expressions of chifa cooking, the unique Peruvian-Chinese cuisine born in Lima in the 19th century. More than 100,000 Chinese contract workers arrived in Peru between 1849 and 1874 to work on the railways and guano fields. They brought wok cooking with them and adapted it using Peruvian beef, local chillies, and native potatoes. The result became a dish so embedded in Peruvian culture that most people no longer think of it as foreign."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
